            <ArticleTitle>Chemotherapy for De Novo Gastric Adenocarcinoma After Deceased Orthotopic Liver Transplantation: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>178-180</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0041-1345(16)30858-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.transproceed.2016.11.024</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">De novo gastric cancer is a rare complication of liver transplantation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Surgical treatments of 16 cases with different outcomes were reported in the English literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">De novo gastric cancer (stage II) was diagnosed as the result of epi-gastric discomfort in a recipient 10 years after liver transplantation. The patient received chemotherapy alone and remained well for 26 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">De novo gastric cancer of liver transplantation recipients is a severe complication; early detection and relevant intervention will benefit these recipients.</AbstractText>
